A||||16||Harry||I really need your input!||||9||
Z||000000||Harry||01-26-2000||09:14 PM||||I started the clutch change out on my GT.<BR>Everything is disconnected, the cradle is on the blocks and guess what happens next?<BR>The back two cradle bolts just plain spin...<BR>Im really torqued, has anybody ever had this one happen?<BR>If I cout the heads off the bolts can I get the old ones out/replace them easy?<BR>Any Idea's from anybody (not on medication) would really be very helpful about now.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Cooter||01-26-2000||09:50 PM||||I was looking under my car and I noticed that the previuos owner must have had the same problem. On the rear bolts, the subframe above the cradle where the bolts go through had a small opening cut into it. It looked just big enough to slip a pair of vise-grips into to hold the spinning nut. Maybe you could also do this.||||reg||1
Z||000002||David Gamache||01-26-2000||09:51 PM||||If you mean the two bolts one on the far right and one on the far left by the exhaust hangers then you do have a bit of a problem. If thses are the problem bolts they bolt into a plate in the frame that is not accessable. Don't panic there is a solution.  Someone may have stripped the bolts in at a previous date or the plate the bolt goes into was damaged. The head of the bolt can be cut and after the cradle is dropped the plate can be re-tapped after the bolts are drilled out. even if the internal plate somehow came loose a small tack weld will hold it, but the stripped bolt problem is the most likely culprit.||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||01-26-2000||09:58 PM||||Ed parks had a solution to this very problem... but I have not archived it.  I forget his actual process.  If anyone would like for me to post the question to one of the lists, I would be glad to do so.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||13
Z||000004||Harry||01-26-2000||10:15 PM||||I was thinking of cutting a hole in the sub frame ( on the outboard side ) right next to the nut at least that way I can replace the bolt and nut assembly. WT III I would appreciate the post if you can get it!<BR>Hopefully I can get this thing down tomorrow and replace this d#$%^ clutch. Its really not bad but the bearing no longer will disengage. So its all coming out.....<BR>Anybody else got any Idea's?||||reg||3
Z||000005||fiero56||01-26-2000||11:11 PM||||I had this same problem just a month or two ago. What you do is(and this is Ed Parks procedure) get a hardened drill bit, and drill holes that connect to each other in a u(looks just like one [_]) around the nut in the frame. Then using pliers bend the 'u' upwards and it exposes the nut. I have pictures but unfortunately no scanner. I know these are really crappy instructions, if you need a picture I can run to kinkos tommorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Harry||01-26-2000||11:18 PM||||How thick was the metal? I'm thinking of using a pneumatic chisel, think it will work as well as drilling?||||reg||3
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||01-27-2000||12:33 AM||||I had the same problem on my 86SE, one of the rear bolts wouldn't come out. The thing the bolts threads into broke the weld(or whatever holds it) and spun with the bolt. The car was being parted due to a few wrecks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> so I grabbed my sawz-all and had some fun.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Harry||01-27-2000||07:53 AM||||Armed with the trusty grinder/drill/chisel I am off to the shop once again to attempt my own personnel holy grail.<BR>More to come tonight......||||reg||4
Z||000009||fiero56||01-27-2000||10:16 AM||||A lot of the problems I had replacing my clutch are in here. Just check the archives and see what other information might be helpfull.||||reg||1
Z||000010||dogtired||01-27-2000||11:23 AM||||Harry:<BR>fiero56 is correct Ed did a write-up on it not too long ago? I do not have it here at work but do have it at home? If you still need it let me know & I will find it!<BR>Per Ed, rust was the big problem??? <BR>The procedure was to cut a U or V shape in the metal, curl it back enough to get on the nut. Replacement involved just a new NUT. After installation they would just bend the opening shut and not weld it???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thats all I remember off the top of my head? Let me know & I will look up the whole procedure tonight???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000011||fiero56||01-27-2000||01:36 PM||||I found it. I knew it was sitting around here somewhere. Here is a copy of the e-mail Ed Parks sent to me:<BR>"Drew, We chisel a 3 sided square in the side of the frame, about 2"x2". Like a "U". Then lift the flap and vise grip the spinning nut cylinder. Use a nut and bolt to reattach and bend the flap back down. We don't weld it in place since that would make it worse next time."<P>When I closed the flap up, I just bent it back down and sealed it up with duct tape. Hope this helps. ||||reg||10
Z||000012||dogtired||01-27-2000||02:30 PM||||Duct Tape and Tye Wraps!<BR>Does Auto Mechanics get any better????    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000013||lowCG||01-27-2000||04:45 PM||||You mentioned that "the bearing won't disengage",do you mean that the clutch mechanism won't disengage?<BR>Just wanted to make sure you were getting full travel at the fork/lever before you consider it a warped disc.<BR>All this talk about rust is making me queezy here in Detriot(saltsahara)with my California car.I'll be sure and flush out that area when I get a chance.<BR>As much of a pain as it sounds,you may have to take the whole subframe out to have a new nutplate welded if you don't have a welder,after grinding/drilling the head of the bolt(s) off.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Harry||01-27-2000||10:35 PM||||Well boy's and girl's,<BR>Yes, rust-rust-rust. My car is originally from Ohio and when I first got it I was amazed at the lack of rust on the floor pans, well now I've found where it was really doing it's damage. My entire cradle is rusty as #$%^&. At any rate, vise grips..No way, Chisel's...no way, Saw's All to the rescue! Had to completely cut the nut and bolt right thru. However I saved the diamond shaped plate and will by new hardware today to replace the old ones.<BR>All I have left to remove is the main wiring to the firewall and my slave line and I can lift the car.<BR>Do I have to remove the console to pull the main harness leads?||||reg||14
Z||000015||Harry||01-27-2000||10:43 PM||||IowCG, Yes I know for a fact that the Throwout bearing is shot. Ive gone thru all the other poop IE slave replacement, checked the arm,yada,yada,yada. Heck my slave pushes so hard you can see the slave move away from the arm (lots-a-travel). Clutch will still kill the motor (bearing's been screeching for a while now, just got worse over time).<BR>Every place here in Norfolk wanted over $300 for clutch/plate/bearing so got mine from Fiero store for about $215 after shipping.<BR>Sorry guy's no kevlar today just cant afford it right now. Heck I still have to pay for some stickers Im ordering for my back lights yet.||||reg||12
Z||000016||dogtired||01-28-2000||04:59 AM||||When I pulled my motor, I pulled the console, unplugged the harness right at the computer and removed the 2 screws that holds the ALDL connector and fed it all through the fire wall.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thats one of the easiest parts of this whole process, only takes a couple minutes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Good Luck!||||reg||6
